api.internal to node2\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"dnsmasq installed and configured on node1 (/etc/dnsmasq.d/internal.conf) with address=/inventory-api.internal/10.100.121.11 (node2's IP), listening on node1's IP with firewalld dns service opened. dig from node1 confirms inventory-api.internal resolves to 10.100.121.11 and external names still resolve via upstream forwarding. This was directly re-verified after two full reboots of node1, including recovery from a discovered boot-race failure, confirming the resolver is durably functional, not just configured.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c4249ce0dcf540f2\",\"cmd-a66f649cd72f4c7c\",\"cmd-df521ff7361c47d9\",\"cmd-39bba404a6714f85\",\"cmd-8da73b0b89ef46e0\",\"cmd-e1a9957dd3a54d79\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node2 is configured to use node1 as its DNS resolver for internal lookups\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node2's NetworkManager connection set ipv4.dns=10.100.121.10 (node1) with ipv4.ignore-auto-dns=yes; /etc/resolv.conf shows nameserver 10.100.121.10. dig/getent on node2 without an explicit @server resolves inventory-api.internal to 10.100.121.11 and external domains successfully, confirming node1 is actually being used as resolver. Re-verified after a full reboot of node2, with resolv.conf and resolution intact.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-2d46286ac0574c00\",\"cmd-795f73d715a74959\",\"cmd-793c23b36f8340b2\",\"cmd-0548bc58d4e146b1\"]}],\"confidence\":0.95,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"All state-changing commands map clearly to the task or to the mandated restart-evidence protocol: dnsmasq/bind-utils/firewalld config on node1, NetworkManager DNS config on node2, and the systemd override.conf drop-in added specifically to fix a real dnsmasq boot-race failure discovered during the first reboot (a corrective action needed to restore functionality, not a gratuitous mutation). The override was verified durable via a second reboot and left in place appropriately. No unnecessary experiments, abandoned config, or unexplained package/service state were found; failed-services list is empty before and after. Minor residue of /tmp/reboot.log and /tmp/reboot2.log from the backgrounded reboot commands is trivial and expected from the restart protocol itself.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b123df44016b4e8d\",\"cmd-e05092ab70d34651\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct, repeated evidence: node1 runs dnsmasq resolving inventory-api.internal to node2's IP while forwarding other queries upstream, and node2 is configured via NetworkManager to use node1 as its DNS resolver, confirmed by resolution tests without an explicit @server flag. The mandatory restart-evidence protocol was executed thoroughly on both nodes, including a real failure (dnsmasq boot-race against DHCP on node1) that was diagnosed, fixed with a systemd override, and then re-validated via a second reboot to confirm durability. node2's single reboot cycle also shows full pre-restart baseline, boot-id change, and post-restart functional recovery.\",\"limitations\":[\"Restart evidence for node1 relies on a self-corrected failure; while the fix was verified across a second reboot, only one instance of the durable (post-fix) boot cycle was captured, so long-term reboot stability beyond two cycles is not directly observed.\",\"No evidence was captured of DNS resolution failing when node1's resolver is down, so resilience/failover behavior beyond simple restart recovery is not assessed (not required by the task contract).\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node1 runs an internal DNS resolver that resolves inventory-api.internal to node2","status":"satisfied","summary":"dnsmasq installed and configured on node1 (/etc/